00:11:20.901 --> 00:11:25.661
two people joined early stage
startups after business school and

00:11:25.661 --> 00:11:28.428
one person did went into venture, and

00:11:28.428 --> 00:11:33.815
then I'm of the four the person who
went on and actually start something.

00:11:33.815 --> 00:11:37.859
And so I think what's great about that
class is that you get a mix of all those,

00:11:37.859 --> 00:11:41.675
it's a big class and so everyone has
a slightly different orientation.

00:11:41.675 --> 00:11:43.568
And so on one end of the spectrum,

00:11:43.568 --> 00:11:47.702
you have folks who just want to
understand what that lean methodology is.

00:11:47.702 --> 00:11:52.050
And on the other hand you have
folks who are 100% new like Ricardo

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:56.409
maybe that he had a specific idea in
mind and wanted to focus on that.

00:11:56.409 --> 00:12:01.241
So that was a really great experience for
me in second year to kind of solidify

00:12:01.241 --> 00:12:05.091
some of the early, to undo kind of
what I had been trained in.

00:12:05.091 --> 00:12:09.502
Which is very process driven, very
buttoned up, very dot all your i's and

00:12:09.502 --> 00:12:13.423
cross all your t's to a startup
mindset of you just try something,

00:12:13.423 --> 00:12:15.542
you throw it out there in the world.

00:12:15.542 --> 00:12:19.200
You get some feedback, you fix it,
you try it again, rinse and

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.627
repeat with a much higher feedback loop.

00:12:21.627 --> 00:12:27.264
And so kind of un-orienting myself from a
previous mode of operation to this new or

00:12:27.264 --> 00:12:31.345
scrappier side is a bit of a transition,
or at least for me,

00:12:31.345 --> 00:12:35.284
it took a little bit of time
to get comfortable with that.

00:12:35.284 --> 00:12:39.608
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and so you mentioned some of your
original group mates now they're all doing

00:12:39.608 --> 00:12:40.676
different things.

00:12:40.676 --> 00:12:45.439
