00:14:53.415 --> 00:14:58.327
So they curate the content to
make it very accessible for

00:14:58.327 --> 00:15:04.284
folks who are going to end up being in
either at a venture capital firm or

00:15:04.284 --> 00:15:06.583
in a corporate setting but

00:15:06.583 --> 00:15:11.620
want to have that entrepreneurial
sort of drive as well.

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:15.441
&gt;&gt; Yes, and while we're on the topic
of experiences and courses,

00:15:15.441 --> 00:15:20.541
Shawon there's a question from Marco here
about kind of prioritizing opportunities

00:15:20.541 --> 00:15:25.535
especially if you're thinking about taking
classes from other places on campus.

00:15:25.535 --> 00:15:29.827
I know you talked a little bit about
that intersection of education,

00:15:29.827 --> 00:15:33.233
can you share a little bit
about how you approach that?

00:15:33.233 --> 00:15:37.778
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I coming into GSB know I
wanted to do one of two things afterwards,

00:15:37.778 --> 00:15:41.909
it was either join an existing
education nonprofit or start my own.

00:15:41.909 --> 00:15:46.402
So I decided to prioritize classes
that would help me do that.

00:15:46.402 --> 00:15:50.432
I personally wasn't looking to explore
that many other topics outside of

00:15:50.432 --> 00:15:53.682
education and social justice
in terms of issue area, and so

00:15:53.682 --> 00:15:58.106
classes that were specifically speaking to
that got to the top of the list for me.

00:15:58.106 --> 00:16:01.085
And then the other choice that I made
around prioritization was around

00:16:01.085 --> 00:16:01.598
skill set.

00:16:01.598 --> 00:16:05.609
So a lot of students in my class were
asking this question, should I focus more

00:16:05.609 --> 00:16:09.860
on the harder technical skills or should
I focus on more of the soft skills like

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:11.770
&gt;&gt; Communication and team management.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:15.243
And I recognized that my bread and butter
was not going to be crunching numbers or

00:16:15.243 --> 00:16:16.686
doing financial analysis, and

00:16:16.686 --> 00:16:19.260
that I just needed the fundamentals
when it came to that.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:22.732
So I leaned more into classes that
were about how can you hire well,

00:16:22.732 --> 00:16:26.390
how can you make sure you have equity
in terms of your company culture?

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:30.133
And how can you actually manage your team,
especially in a remote setting,

00:16:30.133 --> 00:16:33.013
since I knew that might be an option for
for vocal justice.

00:16:33.013 --> 00:16:35.820
So ended up doing lots of education,
nonprofit work and

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.980
the soft skills classes
while I was at GSB.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:41.562
&gt;&gt; And then there's a lot of
really interesting questions.

00:16:41.562 --> 00:16:47.345
There's a lot of interest in that,
the Startup Garage experience and

00:16:47.345 --> 00:16:51.274
a lot of kind of experiential
places on campus.

00:16:51.274 --> 00:16:55.120
How would you talk about
the timing of them?

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.670
Would you do that early on?

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.820
Do they come later in your second year?

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:01.430
I know there's a couple
different paths here, and

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:03.460
then also what's typical at the GSB.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:08.017
Can someone share a little bit, maybe
Jaclyn, since you referenced it first,

00:17:08.017 --> 00:17:11.440
what that timeline could look like for
folks?

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:12.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:18.490
Startup Garage is commonly taken your
second year in the fall quarter.

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:22.144
That's the biggest class, and
then should you choose to continue,

00:17:22.144 --> 00:17:24.820
you can continue on until winter quarter.

00:17:24.820 --> 00:17:29.619
And then some overachievers like Ricardo
could get in winter quarter without having

00:17:29.619 --> 00:17:34.410
done the fall quarter, which is
definitely an option but is less common.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:39.368
And then I believe that engineering
school's lean Launchpad is in the winter,

00:17:39.368 --> 00:17:44.220
quarter and then the D school's
Launchpad is in the spring quarter.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:47.476
Those two classes are more
by application and

00:17:47.476 --> 00:17:52.275
you have to have a team and
have some demonstrated, not traction,

00:17:52.275 --> 00:17:56.400
but demonstrated experimentation
already happening.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:00.664
So it does take some pre-planning
to apply in order to have

00:18:00.664 --> 00:18:04.890
a successful application
to those two classes.

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:09.543
And then in the summer I did something
called Entrepreneurial Summer Program,

00:18:09.543 --> 00:18:12.190
whereby it turned out
at seed stage company.

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:13.930
It was ten people in a room.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:19.136
And Stanford supported part
of my compensation for

00:18:19.136 --> 00:18:22.510
the summer to enable me to work there.

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:24.308
So that's the summer program,

00:18:24.308 --> 00:18:27.910
and I think there's lots of other
resources year round as well.

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:29.330
&gt;&gt; Ricardo or Shawan, for

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:33.668
courses that do involve a group,
did you know the people on the outside?

00:18:33.668 --> 00:18:38.619
What are some different ways to
help you meet people who you might

00:18:38.619 --> 00:18:40.641
be able to work well with?

00:18:40.641 --> 00:18:42.340
&gt;&gt; So I can speak to that.

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:46.680
So I was looking at courses that were
in the social innovation space and

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:50.577
the Center for Social Innovation hosted
lots of mixers, too many to keep up with,

00:18:50.577 --> 00:18:51.778
at the start of the year.

00:18:51.778 --> 00:18:53.293
Where you show up to the event,

00:18:53.293 --> 00:18:56.968
you would actually get different stickers
to represent areas that you were

00:18:56.968 --> 00:19:01.320
passionate about, whether it's education
or youth development or health care.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:04.410
Put those stickers on and
then network with other people that way.

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:07.671
And then through some of those events, you
might meet people who want to support you

00:19:07.671 --> 00:19:09.830
with your venture or
take a class with you.

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:13.198
And then when you're enrolled in some
of the social innovation classes,

00:19:13.198 --> 00:19:16.077
they'll send out a spreadsheet
with ideas that people have and

00:19:16.077 --> 00:19:20.190
then facilitate introductions for you if
you want to get connected to other people.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:23.512
And then if you can't find a group of
people to work with within your class,

00:19:23.512 --> 00:19:26.598
you can always talk to the professor and
pursue your own thing alone.

00:19:26.598 --> 00:19:28.549
And they're really supportive of that,

00:19:28.549 --> 00:19:31.960
which is what I ended up doing in my
Social Innovation practicum course.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:36.360
&gt;&gt; There's a question here from Keyway.

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:41.186
And Shawan, earlier you
mentioned knowing that crunching

00:19:41.186 --> 00:19:46.315
numbers was not necessarily going
to be your bread and butter.

00:19:47.695 --> 00:19:51.182
And as you think about some
of the core experiences and

00:19:51.182 --> 00:19:56.066
skill sets that a leader needs,
it's understanding your own needs versus

00:19:56.066 --> 00:19:59.415
what what things people
on your teams can do.

00:19:59.415 --> 00:20:03.100
And this is a question for
all three of you.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:07.230
What were some skills that you
felt really comfortable with?

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:10.640
And what were ones that you were
looking for others to contribute?

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:13.860
And how did you either discover that?

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:14.860
Was it trial and error?

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:16.880
Did you already know from the outset?

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:18.519
And anybody can jump in on this one.

00:20:21.547 --> 00:20:26.275
&gt;&gt; Yeah, one thing I would say is
just a general sense of drive to

00:20:26.275 --> 00:20:30.825
understand the entrepreneurial
process was important,

00:20:30.825 --> 00:20:36.000
just generally for
people to be bought into the experience.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:39.477
I think that's important and that was
reflected immediately if you met somebody

00:20:39.477 --> 00:20:41.521
that was at one of these
team formation events,

00:20:41.521 --> 00:20:44.750
there's a good chance that they're
trying to figure that out.

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:47.410
And then kind of through discussion and

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:52.241
those events you get a sense of how
much bought into the idea they are.

00:20:52.241 --> 00:20:57.108
The other thing I would say is
around complementary skill sets,

00:20:57.108 --> 00:20:59.970
that was super important as well.

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:02.880
So I have a certain type of skill set.

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:06.190
And I was looking for individuals who
are kind of the opposite of me, right?

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:08.436
Because it doesn't make sense for
me to look for

00:21:08.436 --> 00:21:10.355
folks that are more of me to some extent.

00:21:10.355 --> 00:21:13.383
And that ended up working out really
well for the team that I had for

00:21:13.383 --> 00:21:14.220
Startup Garage.

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:19.139
I'm not the most organized person, but
the partner that I had for Startup Garage

00:21:19.139 --> 00:21:23.280
probably could be an amazing
logistics expert or something right?

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:25.528
So that ended up working out really,
really well.

00:21:26.989 --> 00:21:33.050
&gt;&gt; I would say the traditional answer
I think is to to put it very bluntly,

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:36.772
business people want to find an engineer.

00:21:36.772 --> 00:21:43.859
That was the common trope, if you will,
among founders and I guess the GSB class.

00:21:43.859 --> 00:21:49.001
I attempted to at Stanford,
didn't work out for me, but

00:21:49.001 --> 00:21:56.840
ultimately my two technical co founders
at are people through my GSB network.

00:21:56.840 --> 00:22:01.870
So one was in my past townhouse my second
year he was the only non GSB in our house.

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:05.761
And then the other guy I met
through a GSBer who had known

00:22:05.761 --> 00:22:07.629
him from a previous life.

00:22:07.629 --> 00:22:12.168
So I think there are some people
who felt pressure, I guess,

00:22:12.168 --> 00:22:18.312
to find the perfect person to partner with
and be a co founder with while on campus.

00:22:18.312 --> 00:22:22.470
And I certainly felt that pressure,
but it kind of worked out for

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:26.440
me after the fact as
a result of the GSB network.

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:31.394
So that's kind of additional perspective
I would provide there in addition to what

00:22:31.394 --> 00:22:32.323
Ricardo said.

00:22:32.323 --> 00:22:35.466
Obviously you want people
who are interested and

00:22:35.466 --> 00:22:39.490
aligned with kind of what you
want to put into the class.

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:42.502
Some of the classes I think,
in particular,

00:22:42.502 --> 00:22:46.900
the engineering version of it and
the D school version of it.

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:48.873
Basically everyone has to really commit,

00:22:48.873 --> 00:22:51.820
this is going to be your
top priority that quarter.

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:56.053
The same isn't necessarily true of
Startup Garage, although you could want to

00:22:56.053 --> 00:23:00.970
put yourself in a group where all four of
you kind of are aligned on that point.

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:03.215
But that's a bit more on the, I guess,

00:23:03.215 --> 00:23:06.289
soft side as opposed to like
hard skills assembling.

00:23:06.289 --> 00:23:10.189
&gt;&gt; And you mentioned something really
interesting, Jac, in the network.

00:23:10.189 --> 00:23:14.734
And so thinking about kind of we're
talking a lot about the resources during

00:23:14.734 --> 00:23:16.200
your time at the GSB.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:20.401
But your career and whether you start
entrepreneurship immediately after or

00:23:20.401 --> 00:23:24.070
down the line or your pivot
around a couple different places.

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:27.870
That's like a lifelong path.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:31.789
So, maybe starting with Shawan and
Jaclyn, now that you're out,

00:23:31.789 --> 00:23:35.504
how have the Stanford network
supported you in this endeavor and

00:23:35.504 --> 00:23:39.660
then Ricardo as a current student,
are you experiencing that as well?

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:46.770
And how are the outside post GSB
family members helping you out?

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:49.813
So maybe we can start with Shawan, since-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:49.813 --> 00:23:50.484
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So,

00:23:50.484 --> 00:23:55.983
I am grateful that I have a fellowship
through this Center for Social Innovation,

00:23:55.983 --> 00:24:02.000
Social Innovation Fellowship, that
supports early stage social entrepreneurs.

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:05.310
And through that I get a coach who works
for the Center for Social Innovation.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:08.420
And I meet with her every two
weeks to walk through my idea.

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:11.979
And so that's been incredible
as I'm building an organization,

00:24:11.979 --> 00:24:16.661
hiring my first employee, to have Somebody
who's affiliated with that team to just

00:24:16.661 --> 00:24:19.735
give me real proton feedback and
be a sounding board for me.

00:24:19.735 --> 00:24:22.448
And then the alumni network
is also incredible.

00:24:22.448 --> 00:24:26.891
I have not tapped into it enough because
I'm still breathing after graduating just

00:24:26.891 --> 00:24:27.851
a few months ago.

00:24:27.851 --> 00:24:31.757
But I know that there are tons of
listservs with entrepreneurs affiliated

00:24:31.757 --> 00:24:35.789
with venture studio at Stanford that you
can blast out to if you need advice on

00:24:35.789 --> 00:24:38.510
anything as the emails
coming into it all the time.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:41.686
And so as I'm getting ready to
do fundraising in the future and

00:24:41.686 --> 00:24:45.534
building off the organization more
definitely I'm going to be cold emailing

00:24:45.534 --> 00:24:46.588
a lot of GSB alumni.

00:24:46.588 --> 00:24:50.060
I've heard from friends that the response
rate is really high when you contact

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:51.492
somebody far from the network.

00:24:54.347 --> 00:25:00.399
&gt;&gt; Yeah, just the network is very strong
in my entrepreneurship life, I think

00:25:00.399 --> 00:25:06.276
had I not gone to the GSB I don't think
I would have ended up down this path.

00:25:06.276 --> 00:25:11.499
I would say in terms of kind of,
so my co-founders aren't GSBs, but

00:25:11.499 --> 00:25:17.277
like I said, I met them through my GSB
network like one connection removed.

00:25:17.277 --> 00:25:21.782
I am in a kind of CEO support group
of what among founders in our class,

00:25:21.782 --> 00:25:23.035
and all of us have.

00:25:23.035 --> 00:25:28.218
Some of us are doing venture backed
startups, other folks did search fun.

00:25:28.218 --> 00:25:32.444
So kind of the different types of
the different flavours of entrepreneurship

00:25:32.444 --> 00:25:35.054
that we'll had mentioned
early on the slides.

00:25:35.054 --> 00:25:37.185
And then in addition,

00:25:37.185 --> 00:25:43.250
I've also taken venture funding
from folks in the GSB network.

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:46.635
Several classmates are on my cap table, so

00:25:46.635 --> 00:25:50.497
there's kind of that
financial support as well.

00:25:50.497 --> 00:25:54.625
And so yeah,
definitely the network brings so much or

00:25:54.625 --> 00:26:00.237
it has brought at least so much to
my experience with entrepreneurship.

00:26:00.237 --> 00:26:06.867
It's everywhere and
it's definitely been a huge help today.

00:26:06.867 --> 00:26:13.721
&gt;&gt; And I would just say, while you're
a student, it's still massively impactful.

00:26:13.721 --> 00:26:18.324
I would say the reason I have the
internship I have is because of an MBA2

00:26:18.324 --> 00:26:21.922
that was the intern the previous
year at the same firm.

00:26:21.922 --> 00:26:25.031
And the connections that
I had through them and

00:26:25.031 --> 00:26:29.653
the kind of insight that I had about
the firm and kind of the process and

00:26:29.653 --> 00:26:35.333
the people and everything that was shared
through having that person in my network.

00:26:35.333 --> 00:26:40.010
And then in addition to that, I would
say it's just having being at the GSB,

00:26:40.010 --> 00:26:44.982
especially in the time that you're there
to Shawn's point earlier the response

00:26:44.982 --> 00:26:50.098
rate that you get on emails to individuals
that you reach out to is incredibly high,

00:26:50.098 --> 00:26:50.641
right?

00:26:50.641 --> 00:26:54.044
So of 150 emails that I sent out to VCs,

00:26:54.044 --> 00:26:59.123
I had chats with probably 50
of them right, which is crazy.

00:26:59.123 --> 00:27:04.281
And some of these are partners that
are really important firms and

00:27:04.281 --> 00:27:08.594
those experiences are what
have allowed me to be able to

00:27:08.594 --> 00:27:12.558
have the insights that I have now really,
right?

00:27:12.558 --> 00:27:16.688
And so without that it would be very
difficult to have done that without

00:27:16.688 --> 00:27:21.384
having had a background in venture
entrepreneurship before coming to the GSB.

00:27:21.384 --> 00:27:25.021
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think we're kind
of alluding to this already.

00:27:25.021 --> 00:27:28.477
But maybe if we can distill
even further and take them on,

00:27:28.477 --> 00:27:30.709
we're going to jump into the Q&amp;A box.

00:27:30.709 --> 00:27:34.684
There's a question here from
Chagnon about comparing,

00:27:34.684 --> 00:27:38.585
if you were to go into
entrepreneurship without an MBA.

00:27:38.585 --> 00:27:42.870
How does your GSB experience
bring some unique values or

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:47.805
kind of perspectives to the things
that you're interested in?

00:27:47.805 --> 00:27:52.955
So take a moment, versus no MBA,
or with a now the GSB

00:27:52.955 --> 00:27:57.770
experience, how do you
think it's different?

00:27:57.770 --> 00:28:02.511
Where's the value in pursuing the MBA
if you're thinking about going

00:28:02.511 --> 00:28:04.285
into entrepreneurship?

00:28:04.285 --> 00:28:08.000
This is for anybody on the panel.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:09.423
&gt;&gt; I can kick it off.

00:28:09.423 --> 00:28:11.619
And so a couple things come to mind.

00:28:11.619 --> 00:28:15.348
First, I think that Stanford
teaches entrepreneurship

00:28:15.348 --> 00:28:19.939
in a way that's about the process as
opposed to about a specific idea.

00:28:19.939 --> 00:28:25.498
And I think that is something
that was a mindset shift.

00:28:25.498 --> 00:28:28.206
That was pretty strong for
me because I always thought

00:28:28.206 --> 00:28:31.372
these people started a company
because they had one specific idea.

00:28:31.372 --> 00:28:34.491
And until I got to better pass I recycled.

00:28:34.491 --> 00:28:38.944
There are many, many,
a graveyard of ideas, if you will.

00:28:38.944 --> 00:28:43.022
And this one worked out because
of a series of testing,

00:28:43.022 --> 00:28:47.383
talking to customers,
prototyping that whole process.

00:28:47.383 --> 00:28:50.652
And I think, again,
you can probably read a book and

00:28:50.652 --> 00:28:53.112
understand it's not rocket science.

00:28:53.112 --> 00:28:57.984
But at the same time getting to put
it into practice with mentors along

00:28:57.984 --> 00:29:01.771
the way is the value of being
in a school environment.

00:29:01.771 --> 00:29:06.711
And then the second thing I'd
say is that around the network,

00:29:06.711 --> 00:29:11.188
absolutely kind of already
touched upon that already.

00:29:11.188 --> 00:29:18.161
And that's a huge help to
building your business over time.

00:29:18.161 --> 00:29:22.658
And then after a certain point and I feel
I'm currently making this transition.

00:29:22.658 --> 00:29:27.704
Now is we are a seed stage company,
we're going to grow and now my job has

00:29:27.704 --> 00:29:33.185
quickly transitioned from being just
a scrappy early stage get things done

00:29:33.185 --> 00:29:38.245
as quickly as possible to transitioning
to be a leader of my company.

00:29:38.245 --> 00:29:42.253
And there are a lot of things that,
I mean, I'm not making the transition

00:29:42.253 --> 00:29:46.015
perfectly, but those are different
jobs and different functions.

00:29:46.015 --> 00:29:49.710
And so I think a lot of entrepreneurs
perhaps without their MBA can kind of

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:53.417
chance into because they have a great
idea and it just starts taking off.

00:29:53.417 --> 00:29:58.401
But you might not have the foundational
skills around how to be a good leader,

00:29:58.401 --> 00:30:02.081
how to be a good communicator,
how to build team morale,

00:30:02.081 --> 00:30:06.682
all these things are really crucial
to make your company become a bigger

00:30:06.682 --> 00:30:09.705
company that's going to
stand the test of time.

00:30:09.705 --> 00:30:14.185
And so that final piece of the skill
set is absolutely something that I

00:30:14.185 --> 00:30:17.826
learned at Stanford and
continue to put into practice.

00:30:17.826 --> 00:30:21.642
And I don't think I would have I mean,
again, I could read a book, but

00:30:21.642 --> 00:30:25.747
I don't think it would have sunk in as
much as it did had I not gone to the GSB.

00:30:25.747 --> 00:30:27.756
&gt;&gt; See lots of head nods from the panel.

00:30:27.756 --> 00:30:32.070
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would just add to that,

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:37.847
I think that the sort of being
able to have the time to test and

00:30:37.847 --> 00:30:42.141
try things out was super critical, right?

00:30:42.141 --> 00:30:46.070
So you're spending two years here that you
would otherwise be spending at work or

00:30:46.070 --> 00:30:47.821
whatever and you'd be super busy.

00:30:47.821 --> 00:30:53.517
I can't tell how much time I've had to
be able to reflect on kind of myself and

00:30:53.517 --> 00:30:55.750
the experiences that I have.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:31:00.650
And also try a lot of different things
out in different industries and

00:31:00.650 --> 00:31:06.331
different areas of knowledge that I
wouldn't have had time to explore before.

00:31:06.331 --> 00:31:08.381
And I think what that does for

00:31:08.381 --> 00:31:13.680
the entrepreneurial experiences really
just allow you to do that testing

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:18.563
without kind of any unforeseen kind
of losses or potential losses.

00:31:18.563 --> 00:31:23.850
And allows you to,
Jacly's point to kind of really,

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:27.258
really understand the process and

00:31:27.258 --> 00:31:33.146
not really have to be focused
on the idea itself generally.

00:31:33.146 --> 00:31:37.782
&gt;&gt; And I'd add two things to that one,
there's a joke at GSB that everybody has

00:31:37.782 --> 00:31:42.211
been a startup founder is thinking
about being one will be one, one day.

00:31:42.211 --> 00:31:46.157
And so you're just surrounded by people
who understand that world which, for

00:31:46.157 --> 00:31:49.701
me was really helpful to build my
confidence when it came to pitching.

00:31:49.701 --> 00:31:52.678
There's so many of my friends who
were saying, you should ask for

00:31:52.678 --> 00:31:54.906
even more money for
yourself for the organization.

00:31:54.906 --> 00:31:58.259
And this is how you tactically can go
about doing it, because they understand

00:31:58.259 --> 00:32:01.726
what it's like to put yourself out there
and the importance of making a big ask.

00:32:01.726 --> 00:32:05.029
And I think because I learned that
from people who are a part of this

00:32:05.029 --> 00:32:09.491
entrepreneurship world, they helped us to
secure more funding for the organization.

00:32:09.491 --> 00:32:13.314
And then the second piece is building
on what Ricardo was saying that

00:32:13.314 --> 00:32:16.360
Not only do you have the space
to reflect on ideas, but

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:18.538
you also can get some funding to do so.

00:32:18.538 --> 00:32:19.279
The Center for

00:32:19.279 --> 00:32:23.735
Social Innovation has a fellowship called
the Impact Design Immersion Fellowship.

00:32:23.735 --> 00:32:27.827
For people who want to explore ideas
in the social innovation space between

00:32:27.827 --> 00:32:29.816
their first and second year at GSB.

00:32:29.816 --> 00:32:32.523
And I did that fellowship and
got funding thankfully for

00:32:32.523 --> 00:32:36.150
ten weeks to explore this idea,
talk to students about their experience,

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:37.893
connect with with school leaders.

00:32:37.893 --> 00:32:40.798
And that was awesome just
to learn more about it and

00:32:40.798 --> 00:32:44.743
that's not something I could have
done if I were working full time,

00:32:44.743 --> 00:32:48.843
both because I didn't have the time
to do it or the money to support it.

00:32:48.843 --> 00:32:55.481
&gt;&gt; There's a question here from we've
focused a lot on kind of experience.

00:32:55.481 --> 00:33:01.057
And my suspicion is most of your work
is here in the US kind of domestically,

00:33:01.057 --> 00:33:07.001
but maybe it's in other experiences or
things that your classmates have done.

00:33:07.001 --> 00:33:08.256
Are there resources for

00:33:08.256 --> 00:33:12.151
people looking at startups aiming to
address issues in other countries?

00:33:12.151 --> 00:33:17.535
Any insights on that would be awesome.

00:33:17.535 --> 00:33:22.867
&gt;&gt; Well, first and foremost, I think
the package that you get if you say that

00:33:22.867 --> 00:33:28.285
you have a startup and you're a Stanford
student from the Stanford venture

00:33:28.285 --> 00:33:33.554
studios is blind to where you're
operating, just first and foremost.

00:33:33.554 --> 00:33:37.033
All of the resources that they
put through, for example,

00:33:37.033 --> 00:33:41.151
like connecting you to lawyers or
accountants and things like that.

00:33:41.151 --> 00:33:45.094
They often will make a point to do
things for specific industries and

00:33:45.094 --> 00:33:46.277
specific regions.

00:33:46.277 --> 00:33:51.178
So they would do things for social
innovation, for example specifically, but

00:33:51.178 --> 00:33:53.971
then also for
international things as well.

00:33:53.971 --> 00:33:59.516
So they do have a lot of content that
they cater specifically for that.

00:34:03.611 --> 00:34:09.290
&gt;&gt; Has anybody seen maybe classmates
trying to launch things internationally or

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:11.966
you heard anything from that front?

00:34:11.966 --> 00:34:16.382
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's a company
from my class called Ajaib,

00:34:16.382 --> 00:34:19.652
which is a robo-advisor in Indonesia.

00:34:19.652 --> 00:34:23.162
I think overall, it's a process,
not the idea, and

00:34:23.162 --> 00:34:26.909
that also applies to whether it's US or
international.

00:34:26.909 --> 00:34:30.057
At the end of the day,
you're trying to solve a problem or

00:34:30.057 --> 00:34:31.703
a pain point as it's called.

00:34:31.703 --> 00:34:35.669
And that exists everywhere
in many different forms.

00:34:35.669 --> 00:34:40.591
So it's more about the process
as opposed to a specific idea.

00:34:40.591 --> 00:34:44.467
&gt;&gt; And so kind of on that similar
theme of process versus idea.

00:34:44.467 --> 00:34:50.164
Are folks able to apply the process
to an idea they had before the GSB

00:34:50.164 --> 00:34:56.799
in some of these classes, or are these
ideas that had stuff come up at the GSB?

00:34:56.799 --> 00:35:01.337
&gt;&gt; Can definitely be something you had
before GSB or came up with at GSB.

00:35:01.337 --> 00:35:03.952
I had my idea of a similar version before.

00:35:03.952 --> 00:35:08.827
Actually when I was applying to GSB,
I remember writing about in my application

00:35:08.827 --> 00:35:13.354
and then when I got to GSB I just
started exploring it through coursework.

00:35:13.354 --> 00:35:18.121
Other people set up weekly chats with
friends to just brainstorm a ton of

00:35:18.121 --> 00:35:23.054
ideas and then decide which one they
want to explore while they're there.

00:35:23.054 --> 00:35:25.503
&gt;&gt; My friend Chad Monroe from my class,

00:35:25.503 --> 00:35:29.616
runs a startup called Jupiter
with two of our classmates now.

00:35:29.616 --> 00:35:34.572
When he first got to campus,
I think the original format of his idea

00:35:34.572 --> 00:35:37.948
was shipping things from
the US outside via.

00:35:37.948 --> 00:35:43.285
To bring goods that weren't otherwise
accessible outside the US and

00:35:43.285 --> 00:35:47.149
now it's a last mile grocery
delivery company and

00:35:47.149 --> 00:35:51.672
that evolution kind of took
place while we were at the GSB.

00:35:51.672 --> 00:35:54.996
And en route he got,
two other GSB co founders and

00:35:54.996 --> 00:35:57.681
an engineer from an undergrad I think.

00:35:57.681 --> 00:36:02.466
So anyway all that is to say
you can definitely start early,

00:36:02.466 --> 00:36:07.442
bring people along, look for
people to join you on your ride,

00:36:07.442 --> 00:36:10.139
your idea will probably change.

00:36:10.139 --> 00:36:14.751
And international or US I think
that's all the world is your oyster.

00:36:14.751 --> 00:36:16.607
&gt;&gt; Question about funding.

00:36:16.607 --> 00:36:18.575
So getting resources and

00:36:18.575 --> 00:36:23.737
funding is going to be a big part
of the entrepreneurial journey.

00:36:23.737 --> 00:36:26.136
There's a question from Joshua,

00:36:26.136 --> 00:36:31.442
about resources to help you pitch to
venture capitalists for seed funding.

00:36:31.442 --> 00:36:33.737
Any experiences on that front?

00:36:33.737 --> 00:36:39.299
&gt;&gt; I mean, I would argue that the winter
quarter of Startup Garage is designed

00:36:39.299 --> 00:36:44.701
help you design your pitch deck,
that's the unsaid kind of intention.

00:36:44.701 --> 00:36:49.762
And at the end of that class you
do actually get to pitch live

00:36:49.762 --> 00:36:56.186
to a couple of venture capitalists
that are invited by the teaching team.

00:36:56.186 --> 00:36:58.195
And when I say a couple I
don't mean two or three,

00:36:58.195 --> 00:36:59.960
I mean there was ten of them there, right?

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:04.931
And they're all very related to the area
that you're looking to raise from.

00:37:04.931 --> 00:37:10.123
And from that class there have been a lot
of people who have gone forward and

00:37:10.123 --> 00:37:11.389
raised capital.

00:37:11.389 --> 00:37:16.527
I had the same exact experience
in hacking for defense.

00:37:16.527 --> 00:37:20.579
At the end a bunch of people pitched
some people actually got funded, right?

00:37:20.579 --> 00:37:25.276
So the resources are there and
same thing with Stanford Venture Studios.

00:37:25.276 --> 00:37:29.905
Throughout the year, they'll put out
a lot of resources that you can go and

00:37:29.905 --> 00:37:34.924
look at yourself, to go look at examples
and walkthroughs and things like that.

00:37:34.924 --> 00:37:41.335
But you can also sit down with a VC or
an entrepreneur, and go through your deck.

00:37:41.335 --> 00:37:48.171
We did that on multiple occasions and got
a ton of good feedback from individuals.

00:37:48.171 --> 00:37:53.094
&gt;&gt; And Shawan you mentioned,
you're talking about a mentor.

00:37:53.094 --> 00:37:56.724
Ricardo, you mentioned a mentor
in one of your other classes.

00:37:56.724 --> 00:38:00.217
Do these kind of relationships
come up organically?

00:38:00.217 --> 00:38:04.704
Are they mostly things that come through
structured formats and classrooms?

00:38:04.704 --> 00:38:07.464
How have you seen mentorship,

00:38:07.464 --> 00:38:13.311
advisory sponsor relationships
kind of develop across campus?

00:38:13.311 --> 00:38:15.014
&gt;&gt; For me, it was pretty organic.

00:38:15.014 --> 00:38:19.252
My professor reached out and said, hey, I
wrote a book about founders of nonprofits.

00:38:19.252 --> 00:38:20.734
Do you want to connect with this one?

00:38:20.734 --> 00:38:22.097
And I said, yes, of course I do.

00:38:22.097 --> 00:38:25.834
And we had a call, we hit it off and
then he sent me a really kind of email

00:38:25.834 --> 00:38:29.656
afterwards and so I was like, okay,
this person seems incredible.

00:38:29.656 --> 00:38:33.506
His name's Ray Cyrus and
I wanted to learn from him since his

00:38:33.506 --> 00:38:37.896
nonprofit has done great work in
the education opportunity space.

00:38:37.896 --> 00:38:40.248
And so I just asked him, hey,
do you want to be my mentor for

00:38:40.248 --> 00:38:42.846
the summer while I'm exploring this idea,
and he agreed, and

00:38:42.846 --> 00:38:45.068
then we just continued
working together from there.

00:38:45.068 --> 00:38:48.712
I think other people might do
some cold emails to find mentors

00:38:48.712 --> 00:38:53.427
in a more formal sense, but for me it was
just organically talking to people and

00:38:53.427 --> 00:38:56.512
then see who you connect with and
going from there.

00:38:56.512 --> 00:39:01.299
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so there are formal processes for
it that I went through and

00:39:01.299 --> 00:39:07.057
Startup Garage and Hacking for Defense
where you get matched up with a mentor.

00:39:07.057 --> 00:39:11.099
Those are also hit or miss, just like
in normal life, if you go out and

00:39:11.099 --> 00:39:15.298
try to find a mentor, it can work
sometimes and sometimes it doesn't.

00:39:15.298 --> 00:39:19.026
We were fortunate that one
of the folks that was one of

00:39:19.026 --> 00:39:23.958
our mentors was amazing and
we hit it off and the sessions were great.

00:39:23.958 --> 00:39:27.710
But there were examples when
that wasn't quite the case.

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:32.473
So there are a lot of examples
that are more structured, but

00:39:32.473 --> 00:39:35.055
there are to Shawann's point.

00:39:35.055 --> 00:39:39.737
Probably the person who's helped me
the most is somebody that I reached out

00:39:39.737 --> 00:39:40.935
to via cold email.

00:39:40.935 --> 00:39:43.043
I talked to a whole bunch of people but

00:39:43.043 --> 00:39:46.172
that person was the one that
I connected with the most.

00:39:46.172 --> 00:39:50.696
And I just kept having more questions for
and kept connecting with.

00:39:50.696 --> 00:39:55.382
And they agreed to and they provided all
sorts of guidance and things like that.

00:39:55.382 --> 00:39:58.470
I think it can happen
very organically as well.

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:03.918
Just if you're willing to be coached,
you'll find someone.

00:40:03.918 --> 00:40:04.720
&gt;&gt; Jaclyn, here's a question for you.

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:08.740
How did you think about
the cost of the MBA?

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:13.295
So we're taking a whole left turn here
kind of coming back to the beginning of

00:40:13.295 --> 00:40:14.158
the journey.

00:40:14.158 --> 00:40:19.554
How did you think about the cost of the
MBA, including opportunity cost of living,

00:40:19.554 --> 00:40:25.090
lucrative career, finance, In the context
of wanting to start a company,

00:40:25.090 --> 00:40:27.684
how did you kind of
approach that mentally?

00:40:27.684 --> 00:40:32.524
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm not doing a startup because
I think it's going to make me a ton

00:40:32.524 --> 00:40:33.271
of money.

00:40:33.271 --> 00:40:38.194
I mean, that would be great as a side
effect, but the reason I'm doing it

00:40:38.194 --> 00:40:43.143
is I think it's the best conduit for
personal and professional growth.

00:40:43.143 --> 00:40:47.085
The actual content, I deal with IRS
rules on taxability of benefits.

00:40:47.085 --> 00:40:51.277
When I was five years old, I did not
dream, it's not like going to space,

00:40:51.277 --> 00:40:52.574
that was not my dream.

00:40:52.574 --> 00:40:58.250
But the lessons learned along the way are
why I wanted to do it to start with and

00:40:58.250 --> 00:41:02.437
that's what sustains me when
things get really tough.

00:41:02.437 --> 00:41:07.358
And your first five or ten customer calls
and they tell you your idea's not great,

00:41:07.358 --> 00:41:09.994
or they would never buy it or
whatever it is,

00:41:09.994 --> 00:41:13.233
I think that's that's what
sustains me through it.

00:41:13.233 --> 00:41:17.937
So I didn't think of starting
a company in a rational way,

00:41:17.937 --> 00:41:24.381
I would say that you're presenting it
that way, it was never for me about that.

00:41:24.381 --> 00:41:28.335
I think even if I had taken the money
I invested in my MBA and said,

00:41:28.335 --> 00:41:32.380
I'm going to put it in a bank account and
start spending that money.

00:41:32.380 --> 00:41:36.198
I don't think I would have had a clue on
how to actually do that in a good way.

00:41:36.198 --> 00:41:37.920
And like I said, I didn't have an idea.

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:39.121
I didn't have a process.

00:41:39.121 --> 00:41:40.749
I didn't have a cofounder.

00:41:40.749 --> 00:41:45.512
So those are other very important
ingredients other than money for

00:41:45.512 --> 00:41:48.331
really to start something of your own.

00:41:48.331 --> 00:41:53.733
So I guess, yeah, my answer is like
I didn't think about it in that way.

00:41:53.733 --> 00:41:56.569
And if you have a fully
fleshed out idea and

00:41:56.569 --> 00:41:59.738
all you really need is
capital to make it work.

00:41:59.738 --> 00:42:03.168
Yeah, I mean,
I think you can make the case for that.

00:42:03.168 --> 00:42:09.236
But for me it was about getting the other
ingredients for starting a company.

00:42:09.236 --> 00:42:11.779
&gt;&gt; And here's a question from Michael.

00:42:11.779 --> 00:42:15.119
Thinking about opportunities
to continue working or

00:42:15.119 --> 00:42:19.728
launching something outside of
the classroom during the academic year.

00:42:19.728 --> 00:42:20.954
Is that possible?

00:42:20.954 --> 00:42:24.655
How do you juggle your time?

00:42:24.655 --> 00:42:29.221
Were you continuing to work on your ideas
outside of the classroom after those

00:42:29.221 --> 00:42:30.706
classes have been over?

00:42:33.027 --> 00:42:34.139
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely.

00:42:34.139 --> 00:42:35.832
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sorry.

00:42:35.832 --> 00:42:40.820
Yeah, certainly,
I think it's kind of you manage

00:42:40.820 --> 00:42:44.648
your own time the way that you want to and

00:42:44.648 --> 00:42:49.880
you kind of prioritize things
as you see fit, right?

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:54.683
I think you are advised not to
work at a startup while you're

00:42:54.683 --> 00:42:59.303
doing your MBA, but
there are people that do it, right?

00:42:59.303 --> 00:43:03.744
So people have done it and they have great
experiences and kind of learned a lot and

00:43:03.744 --> 00:43:05.456
have a lot of personal growth.

00:43:05.456 --> 00:43:08.394
And then there's tons of people
that are working on ideas,

00:43:08.394 --> 00:43:11.275
whether they're doing it
through coursework or not, and

00:43:11.275 --> 00:43:13.945
they spend a lot of time
outside of school doing that.

00:43:13.945 --> 00:43:18.812
And being around your classmates
helps quite a bit in terms of

00:43:18.812 --> 00:43:24.075
breaking down barriers that you're
facing and all that as well.

00:43:27.407 --> 00:43:30.092
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that hits on a lot of
what I was going to bring up.

00:43:30.092 --> 00:43:35.572
It is hard to work on your idea and
also be a student and have a social life.

00:43:35.572 --> 00:43:38.778
But I think if you come in and
you say this is what I want to do, and

00:43:38.778 --> 00:43:42.543
I know I'm committed to at least
explain the idea, you'd block the time

00:43:42.543 --> 00:43:46.270
on your calendar before somebody else
sends you an invite for an event.

00:43:46.270 --> 00:43:47.893
And for me it was worth it.

00:43:47.893 --> 00:43:52.585
I said no to a lot of stuff at GSB so I
could be off campus, going to schools, and

00:43:52.585 --> 00:43:53.538
pilot my idea.

00:43:53.538 --> 00:43:57.273
But in the long run in line with
what I wanted to do after GSB and

00:43:57.273 --> 00:44:01.238
I still had time to make good friends and
have fun and all of that.

00:44:01.238 --> 00:44:03.199
So it's not like you sacrifice everything,

00:44:03.199 --> 00:44:05.063
you just have to be
a bit more intentional.

00:44:05.063 --> 00:44:08.717
The other thing is that there are a lot of
classes where you can work on your venture

00:44:08.717 --> 00:44:10.018
and do things for the class.

00:44:10.018 --> 00:44:14.377
So, basically, any class that I was in,
I found a way to tie typical justice.

00:44:14.377 --> 00:44:17.192
So I was in winning writing that
was a communications course,

00:44:17.192 --> 00:44:19.369
I would have my pitches
be about Vocal Justice.

00:44:19.369 --> 00:44:23.203
Or my nonprofit management class, I would
do research on a competitor that I needed

00:44:23.203 --> 00:44:25.026
to research anyway for Vocal Justice.

00:44:25.026 --> 00:44:30.564
And so it ends up being less work in the
end, since you have a dual purpose for it.

00:44:30.564 --> 00:44:34.081
&gt;&gt; Then Shawan,
did you do a low keynote as well?

00:44:34.081 --> 00:44:35.669
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, I did low keynote,

00:44:35.669 --> 00:44:38.335
which is a nine minute talk on
the issue you care about and

00:44:38.335 --> 00:44:40.054
mine was related to Vocal Justice.

00:44:40.054 --> 00:44:43.861
It was on public speaking and
critical consciousness and

00:44:43.861 --> 00:44:46.801
I use that now to promote
the organization.

00:44:46.801 --> 00:44:47.993
&gt;&gt; These are also accessible.

00:44:47.993 --> 00:44:49.996
So if you go to YouTube and
search low keynotes,

00:44:49.996 --> 00:44:51.608
you can also see what that looks like.

00:44:51.608 --> 00:44:54.507
So encourage folks to
check that out as well.

00:44:57.139 --> 00:44:58.232
And Jaclyn, did you pop off?

00:44:58.232 --> 00:45:00.233
Did you have a?

00:45:00.233 --> 00:45:02.475
&gt;&gt; Nope, nothing
&gt;&gt; Awesome, and so

00:45:02.475 --> 00:45:05.623
we're talking about
intentionality with time.

00:45:05.623 --> 00:45:11.452
I think that can also be
an intentionality with opportunities.

00:45:11.452 --> 00:45:16.241
Question from Jay is how did you
balance driving towards that idea

00:45:16.241 --> 00:45:19.908
versus staying open and
exploring other things?

00:45:19.908 --> 00:45:24.771
Any advice to someone who's
grappling with that same tension?

00:45:31.451 --> 00:45:36.960
&gt;&gt; I think by being around classmates
who are doing lots of different things,

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:39.416
you're by default kind of open.

00:45:39.416 --> 00:45:42.376
Just from lunchtime,
dinnertime conversation,

00:45:42.376 --> 00:45:46.174
what's everyone interested in,
what's everyone focused on?

00:45:46.174 --> 00:45:51.876
And so you'll just learn by default a lot
of what other pursuits could look like.

00:45:51.876 --> 00:45:58.117
So guess, for me, I felt like it was very
clear what the different flavors of and

00:45:58.117 --> 00:46:02.871
if anything, I could draw
inspiration from my classmates of

00:46:02.871 --> 00:46:06.058
how they were pursuing it and their way.

00:46:06.058 --> 00:46:10.798
That gave me ideas on how I
might inform my own process.

00:46:10.798 --> 00:46:12.083
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:46:12.083 --> 00:46:14.936
&gt;&gt; One thing I would
just add there quickly,

00:46:14.936 --> 00:46:19.747
as long as you're committed to sort
of either being an entrepreneur or

00:46:19.747 --> 00:46:23.516
joining a startup,
I think things become way easier.

00:46:23.516 --> 00:46:28.001
Because if you are also trying to recruit
for consulting and PE, then things

00:46:28.001 --> 00:46:32.712
become more difficult because you're
basically recruiting the entire year.

00:46:32.712 --> 00:46:35.493
But if you commit to kind of
being either an entrepreneur or

00:46:35.493 --> 00:46:38.921
going to work at a startup then you
have a ton of time to explore, right?

00:46:38.921 --> 00:46:41.105
You don't have to commit
to something immediately.

00:46:41.105 --> 00:46:43.731
Or if you do commit to something for
a month, and

00:46:43.731 --> 00:46:45.832
you decide to pivot at the end of that,

00:46:45.832 --> 00:46:50.188
then it's not a waste of time because
you still have more months to go, right?

00:46:50.188 --> 00:46:52.973
So I wouldn't be too concerned about that.

00:46:52.973 --> 00:46:55.691
It's more just making
the decision between am

00:46:55.691 --> 00:46:58.972
I going to go the traditional
kind of consulting PE route?

00:46:58.972 --> 00:47:03.782
Or am I going to go
the entrepreneurship startup route?

00:47:03.782 --> 00:47:05.950
&gt;&gt; And we're getting close to time.

00:47:05.950 --> 00:47:09.481
And so I'm going to go ahead and
claim this last question.

00:47:09.481 --> 00:47:12.895
As you're thinking,
words of wisdom to a younger you,

00:47:12.895 --> 00:47:17.707
whether it's in the application process,
it's during your time at the GSB.

00:47:17.707 --> 00:47:20.407
Maybe it's just as you graduated.

00:47:20.407 --> 00:47:25.000
As you're venturing out onto this
whole new brave world ahead.

00:47:25.000 --> 00:47:28.042
What advice would you give to yourself?

00:47:28.042 --> 00:47:33.610
So, take a moment and then maybe,
Ricardo we could start with you and

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:36.497
Shawan then Jaclyn to take us home.

00:47:39.694 --> 00:47:44.298
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say I mean first and
foremost, breathe, right?

00:47:44.298 --> 00:47:48.368
It's a bit of a long process and
it's super fun.

00:47:48.368 --> 00:47:54.976
It's really transformative from
a personal and educational perspective.

00:47:54.976 --> 00:47:57.805
You're going to meet a ton
of amazing people who you

00:47:57.805 --> 00:48:01.600
never thought you would be kind
of rubbing elbows with and stuff.

00:48:01.600 --> 00:48:03.334
But just breathe for now and

00:48:03.334 --> 00:48:06.735
just know that the the world's
going to be your oyster.

00:48:06.735 --> 00:48:11.314
Once you're here you're going to have
tons of opportunities to meet people,

00:48:11.314 --> 00:48:16.250
have conversations you'd never thought
you'd have and learn topics that you'd

00:48:16.250 --> 00:48:20.157
never thought you'd kind of be diving into
&gt;&gt; Okay, Shawan.

00:48:20.157 --> 00:48:21.582
I would say take time for

00:48:21.582 --> 00:48:25.153
yourself and I think there
are two ways to think about that.

00:48:25.153 --> 00:48:30.292
One is just time for you to breathe,
relax, binge watch the show on Netflix.

00:48:30.292 --> 00:48:34.223
Because entrepreneurship journey is hectic
and so you got to just take that time and

00:48:34.223 --> 00:48:36.314
be diligent about taking care of yourself.

00:48:36.314 --> 00:48:41.396
But the other way I think about
that is time for introspection.

00:48:41.396 --> 00:48:45.253
I think a lot of my best thinking comes
when I'm just sitting by myself for

00:48:45.253 --> 00:48:49.504
a couple of hours and free writing about
why I'm doing the work that I'm doing.

00:48:49.504 --> 00:48:51.799
What my values are,
what some of my concerns are, and

00:48:51.799 --> 00:48:52.821
how I want to get better.

00:48:52.821 --> 00:48:57.066
And it's really hard to make that time
on your own if you're just go, go,

00:48:57.066 --> 00:48:58.370
go with your venture.

00:48:58.370 --> 00:49:02.018
And so being diligent about having
that time maybe every two weeks,

00:49:02.018 --> 00:49:05.290
every month to have some
introspection thing is important.

00:49:05.290 --> 00:49:08.469
&gt;&gt; Awesome and then,
Jacqueline, taking us home.

00:49:08.469 --> 00:49:12.040
&gt;&gt; Well, the first, Ricardo and
Sean's answers were both really good.

00:49:12.040 --> 00:49:14.670
It's all going to be fine.

00:49:14.670 --> 00:49:20.417
I would say have a plan and be patient.

00:49:20.417 --> 00:49:27.651
So I think for me having a few areas of
focus helped me make easier decisions

00:49:27.651 --> 00:49:33.617
given just the wealth of things
you could be doing at the GSB.

00:49:33.617 --> 00:49:38.067
And your calendar will be booked
end to end and probably double book

00:49:38.067 --> 00:49:42.211
because there's so many cool
things that you want to be doing.

00:49:42.211 --> 00:49:47.217
And so you need some level of focus
of what you want your experience

00:49:47.217 --> 00:49:53.418
to be oriented around, a few things, and
really spend time on those two things.

00:49:53.418 --> 00:49:58.248
And then finally, I would say I think one
sense of feeling I have is like, I see so

00:49:58.248 --> 00:50:01.037
and so doing this and
that's really taking off or

00:50:01.037 --> 00:50:04.256
I see so and so doing this and
that's really taking off.

00:50:04.256 --> 00:50:09.222
And I think in hindsight having a bit
more patience with myself that for

00:50:09.222 --> 00:50:14.284
me my entrepreneurship journey
bloomed kind of post business school.

00:50:14.284 --> 00:50:19.562
And I wasn't one of the ones that kind
of came in with a clear vision in mind.

00:50:19.562 --> 00:50:23.460
But it still kind of worked out
the way that it was meant to work out.

00:50:23.460 --> 00:50:26.237
So be patient and have a plan.

00:50:26.237 --> 00:50:31.659
&gt;&gt; Wonderful, so breathe, take time for
yourself, be patient, have a plan.

00:50:31.659 --> 00:50:36.042
These are [LAUGH] all really
wise words to live by.

00:50:36.042 --> 00:50:39.967
Definitely stuff that I'm going to be
applying to my own life even though I'm

00:50:39.967 --> 00:50:42.102
